SIGNWRITER'S QUOTE

Signwriter's Quote — a real-world task using only today's concepts: for loops, looping over text one character at a time, counting with variables inside a loop, reusing comparisons, if / elif / else, variables, input(), arithmetic, and printing with 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A signwriter on Trans Amadi Road quotes for shop signboards by character: N150 for every character painted, but spaces are free - they cost nothing to paint. Before he starts work he runs the phrase through a small program that walks through the text character by character and prices the job.

The program must:
- Ask for the signboard phrase.
- Walk through the text one character at a time, counting: the total characters, and how many of them are spaces.
- Print exactly three lines:
  CHARACTERS: 9 (2 spaces)
  BILLABLE: 7
  QUOTE: N1050.00
- Billable characters = total characters minus spaces. Quote = billable x 150, in naira with two decimal places.
- Do NOT use len() for this - the whole point is counting the characters yourself inside the loop.

Sample run:

Signboard phrase: Ada Moore

CHARACTERS: 9 (1 space)
BILLABLE: 8
QUOTE: N1200.00

Edge cases to handle: an empty phrase (just Enter - zero characters, quote N0.00), a phrase made only of spaces (billable 0, quote N0.00), a single-character phrase, and a long phrase with no spaces at all.
